@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# Direct Mode — SpoolmanSync without Home Assistant
+
+This is a fork of [gibz104/SpoolmanSync](https://github.com/gibz104/SpoolmanSync)
+(MIT licensed — see `LICENSE.txt`), with one addition: a way to connect
+straight to a printer's Moonraker API instead of going through Home
+Assistant + `ha_creality_ws`. Everything else — the dashboard, spool
+matching, low-stock alerts, QR/label printing, reports — is unmodified
+upstream SpoolmanSync.
+
+If you already have Home Assistant, use upstream's normal HA modes instead —
+they have RFID auto-match, which direct mode doesn't (see below). Direct
+mode exists specifically for people who don't run Home Assistant and don't
+want to add it just for this.
+
+## What changed, file by file
+
+- `app/src/lib/api/moonraker-direct.ts` — queries a printer's `box` and
+  `print_stats` Moonraker objects directly over HTTP and maps the result
+  into the same `HAPrinter`/`HATray`/`HAAMS` shapes `lib/api/homeassistant.ts`
+  produces, so nothing downstream (dashboard rendering, spool matching,
+  mismatch detection) needed to change.
+- `app/src/lib/direct-poller.ts` — replaces the role Home Assistant
+  automations normally play (watching for tray/print state changes and
+  POSTing `spool_usage` events to `/api/webhook`). This polls each
+  configured printer's CFS slots directly, and when a slot's remaining
+  length has dropped since the last poll, POSTs the same webhook payload an
+  HA automation would have sent.
+- `app/src/lib/direct-printers.ts` — stores the list of directly-connected
+  printers (name + Moonraker URL) in the existing generic `Settings`
+  key/value table. No database schema migration was needed.
+- `app/src/lib/material-names.ts` — a small material-code→name table, since
+  there's no `ha_creality_ws` doing that translation in direct mode.
+- `app/src/instrumentation.ts` — boots the poller when the server starts.
+- `app/src/app/direct-printers/page.tsx` — a page for adding/removing direct
+  printers.
+- `app/src/app/api/printers/route.ts`, `app/src/app/api/settings/route.ts`,
+  `app/src/components/nav.tsx` — small additive patches so the above plugs
+  into the existing discovery/settings/nav flow.
+- `docker-compose.direct.yml` — runs just the SpoolmanSync app container, no
+  Home Assistant container at all.
+
+## Setup
+
+1. `docker compose -f docker-compose.direct.yml up -d`
+2. Open `http://<this-host>:3000`, go to **Settings**, connect your existing
+   Spoolman instance.
+3. Go to **Direct Printers**, add your printer: a name and its Moonraker
+   URL (typically `http://<printer-ip>:7125`).
+4. The dashboard now discovers CFS slots straight from that printer. Assign
+   spools by clicking a slot, same as upstream.
+
+Adding at least one printer here switches printer discovery for the whole
+app to direct mode — `/api/printers` stops calling Home Assistant entirely
+once a direct printer is configured. Removing all direct printers falls
+back to the Home Assistant path automatically.
+
+## What this does NOT give you, compared to the Home Assistant path
+
+**RFID auto-match doesn't work in direct mode.** Upstream's
+`ha_creality_ws`-based path can read a spool's RFID/serial number and
+automatically recognise a physical spool it's seen before. The Moonraker
+`box` object (per the community documentation this fork relies on) does not
+confirm exposing that serial number — only material code, colour, and
+remaining length. Without it, spool assignment in direct mode is
+manual-click or material/colour matching only. If a future firmware or
+Moonraker component exposes the tag serial through `box` or another object,
+`lib/api/moonraker-direct.ts`'s `slotToTray()` is the one place to add it.
+
+**Per-slot usage tracking is new code, not a port of anything upstream.**
+`lib/direct-poller.ts` was written for this fork. It assumes a CFS slot's
+`remain_len` decreases monotonically during a print and only increases when
+a spool is swapped — a reasonable assumption, not a confirmed one. It polls
+every 30 seconds by default (`DIRECT_POLL_INTERVAL_MS`), so usage is
+attributed in discrete chunks, not in real time. This has been verified by
+unit tests and a full `next build`/`tsc`/`vitest` pass in isolation — it has
+**not** been run against a real printer. Watch the **Logs** page after your
+first few prints to confirm deductions look right before trusting it
+unattended.
+
+**The `box`/`M8200` schema itself is confirmed on K2 Plus, not K2 Pro.**
+Same caveat as the OpenFilamentCFS Android project this fork is a companion
+to: the field names (`color_value`, `material_type`, `remain_len`, `state`,
+`auto_refill`) come from community reverse-engineering documented as
+confirmed on K2 Plus; K2/K2 Pro are marked untested in that source. If your
+K2 Pro reports different field names, `lib/api/moonraker-direct.ts` is a
+single, small file to adjust — that was the point of keeping it separate
+from `lib/api/homeassistant.ts` rather than patching HA-discovery logic
+in place.
+
+## Relationship to OpenFilamentCFS
+
+This fork's `lib/api/moonraker-direct.ts` uses the same `box` object schema
+(and the same 41-entry material-code table) as the OpenFilamentCFS Android
+app's `MoonrakerClient.kt` and `MaterialCodes.kt` — both trace back to the
+same community documentation, kept consistent on purpose. OpenFilamentCFS
+writes the serial-number-as-Spoolman-spool-ID convention into a tag at
+tagging time; that convention is what upstream's RFID auto-match consumes.
+Direct mode can't consume it (see above) until the tag serial is
+independently confirmed available from Moonraker — at which point it should
+be usable the same way here.
